    {
      "id": "scene-02b",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "heading": "What Does 'Directly Proportional' Mean?",
        "bullets": [
          "Voltage (V) is the PUSH that forces charges to move.",
          "Current (I) is the FLOW RATE of those charges.",
          "More Push = More Flow! Double the Voltage → Current Doubles.",
          "The ratio V / I stays CONSTANT for a conductor. That constant is Resistance (R)."
        ],
        "imageUrl": "/images/resistor_water_analogy.jpg"
      },
      "teacherScript": "What does directly proportional mean in simple terms? Think of Voltage as the PUSH, and Current as the FLOW. If you double the push by adding a battery, the flow of electric current doubles too! The ratio of Voltage to Current always stays the exact same constant value—and that constant is Resistance.",
